# ── Numeric-comparison boundary pins ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# KIND: MECHANISM pin — fixture inline by design, see pins/rights-floor.nibli.
#
# Run by `just verify-pins` (nibli-pin, native).
#
# WHAT THIS GUARDS. `greater` / `less` / `num_equal` live on BOTH sides of a seam,
# and which side an atom lands on is decided by its OPERANDS, not by its name:
#
#   greater(20, 15).      numeric  — a query COMPUTES it; the store never holds it
#   greater(Alis, Bob).   relational — "taller than"; an ordinary stored fact
#
# The first spelling is refused in every ASSERTED position (ground fact, rule
# antecedent, negated antecedent, rule head). That refusal is not tidiness. A rule
# lowers its atoms to store-lookup templates, so an asserted comparison is inert as
# a positive guard, and under `~` it succeeds for EVERY binding because nothing is
# stored — concluding the head for exactly the subjects whose value really was
# greater. A definitive wrong TRUE, which is the one failure class this engine
# exists to prevent.
#
# The second spelling must survive untouched, because banning three relation names
# would take a legitimate relational predicate with it. That asymmetry is the whole
# design, and nothing in the surface syntax shows it — which is why it is pinned
# here rather than left to the tests that happen to cover it today.
#
# DECIDED, NOT DEFERRED (2026-08-08). Making a rule-position comparison compute on
# bound values was evaluated and declined: it is a group-level rewrite of rule
# compilation, the guard cannot be made position-aware where it runs, and NEITHER
# differential oracle can judge arithmetic — so the feature would ship unchecked by
# the two gates that exist to catch this class. GUARANTEES §Disclosed Sharp Edges
# carries the re-open trigger.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE CANNOT PIN. The pin language has `? <query>` and a verdict, but no
# find/count/aggregate form. That the boolean verdict AGREES with witness
# enumeration — the defect fixed alongside this decision — is pinned in Rust
# (`verdict_and_find_agree_for_every_comparison`, nibli-reason; and
# `numeric_threshold_verdict_and_find_agree`, nibli-engine). Do not read this file
# as covering it.

# ── THE COMPUTED READING IS REFUSED IN EVERY ASSERTED POSITION ────────────────
# All four carry the same needle, so a typo (a Syntax error) cannot satisfy them.

# A ground fact. Nothing would ever consult it: the query recomputes.
:refuse reasoning /computed comparison/
greater(3, 1).

# A positive rule guard. This is the "threshold rule" a reader reaches for first.
:refuse reasoning /computed comparison/
all $x: all $n: person($x) & quantity($x, $n) & greater($n, 15) -> fit($x).

# A NEGATED rule guard — the dangerous one. Refused, so it can never overfire.
:refuse reasoning /computed comparison/
all $x: all $n: person($x) & quantity($x, $n) & ~greater($n, 15) -> rotten($x).

# A rule HEAD. Dead on arrival: computation runs before any store lookup.
:refuse reasoning /computed comparison/
all $x: all $n: quantity($x, $n) -> greater($n, 100).

# A variable is refused as readily as a literal — it COULD bind to a number, and
# the guard is conservative in the safe direction.
:refuse reasoning /computed comparison/
all $a: all $b: person($a) & less($a, $b) -> fit($a).

# ── THE RELATIONAL READING IS NOT COLLATERAL DAMAGE ───────────────────────────
# Non-numeric operands mean store lookup on BOTH sides, so the two halves cannot
# disagree and the fact asserts normally. These are premises for the queries below,
# so they stay resident deliberately (not `:accept-scoped`).

:accept
greater(Alis, Bob).

? greater(Alis, Bob).
# => TRUE

# Not asserted, and no rule concludes it — the closed-world answer, not an error.
? greater(Bob, Alis).
# => FALSE

# And it still works as a rule guard, which is the part a name-based ban would
# have destroyed.
person(Ara).

:accept
all $x: person($x) & greater(Alis, Bob) -> fit($x).

? fit(Ara).
# => TRUE

# ── THE QUERY SIDE IS WHERE A COMPARISON BELONGS ──────────────────────────────
# Arithmetic, not lookup: a FALSE here is decided, not an absence of evidence.

? greater(5, 3).
# => TRUE

? greater(3, 5).
# => FALSE

? less(3, 5).
# => TRUE

? num_equal(5, 5).
# => TRUE

? num_equal(5, 3).
# => FALSE

# ── THE HONEST BOUNDARY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There is no numeric threshold RULE, and this file is where that is stated rather
# than discovered. The two idioms that cover the real cases:
#
# (1) Assert the CLASSIFICATION the source states and let rules read that. This is
#     what `drug-interactions.nibli` does with `thin` (narrow therapeutic index) —
#     a clinical judgement, not a workaround for a missing comparison.
thin(Varfarin).

:accept
all $d: thin($d) -> dangerous($d).

? dangerous(Varfarin).
# => TRUE

# (2) For ORDER rather than magnitude, the numeral-free time relations already
#     carry it — see pins/temporal-order.nibli. Nothing here reopens that door:
#     a magnitude may be stated as an opaque term, but no arithmetic follows from
#     it, and no rule may compare two of them.

:expect-pins 17
